f 17592186045417] [17592186305140 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305140 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305140 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305140 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305140 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{-3x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305140 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51ccf9c3-0db9-5b22-9dee-31cd44f9c3bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305140 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+12{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta +12\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha +12\\beta =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha +12\\beta =11\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{19}{24},\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{19}{24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+(-\\frac{19}{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(\\frac{1}{8})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342732 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342732 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342732 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3bdb4010-1e08-5378-a110-856c1b0d31e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343418 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343418 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343418 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8844755e-17ca-5997-8087-0445418bac62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343570 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty